  "query_preview": "..A large upper low continues to \nslowly move east through the Pacific NW, deepening with eastern \nextent. A tight southwest-northeast oriented pressure gradient \nremains in place, tightest across western UT resulting in another \nafternoon of enhanced southwesterly flow overhead. Most areas across \nUT and SW WY can once again expect to see gusts in the 25-35mph \nrange, though areas west of I-15 may see gusts approach (and \npotentially exceed) 45mph given stronger winds aloft with higher \nsurface temperatures to encourage stronger mixing. \n\nThis afternoon, moisture associated with Hurricane Marie will phase \ninto the mean upper flow as as the southern stream nudges north and \nincreases in magnitude. PWAT anomalies on the order of 0.5-0.8 above \nseasonal normals (potentially exceeding 200% normal values) are \nforecast to push north across much of Utah with this increase in \nsouthwesterly flow. Currently, SPC MesoAnalysis suggests that PWATs \non the order of 0.8-1.2\" of water have already begun to push into \nsouthern UT. Widespread ascent will strengthen as the southern \nstream phases into the mean flow with the aforementioned upper low, \nwith a quick eastern progression of the main precipitation axis \nthrough the afternoon hours. As such, widespread showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast to continue developing across central and \nsouthern UT. Northern UT will also see precipitation this afternoon, \nthough it is more likely due to frontogenetical forcing ahead of \nan incoming frontal boundary. Despite enhanced flow aloft \nsuggesting fast moving storms, robust moisture content within the \natmosphere continues to yield concern for flash flooding across \ncentral and southern UT, where a slight risk (level 2/4) for \nexcessive rainfall has been outlined by the Weather Prediction \nCenter (WPC). Rainfall totals have decreased across CAM guidance, \nsuggesting lower rates with 0.3-0.5\"/hr suggested on the higher \nend. Despite this, some instances of flash flooding remain \nexpected given the anomalously moist airmass in place. \n\nRegarding the severe threat, the forecast remains on track regarding \nan isolated threat across far NW UT. Ample shear exists on the order \nof 40-50kts ahead of the incoming frontal boundary, though only \nmodest buoyancy develops in the midst of lower surface temperatures \nand drier air ahead of the front. The main hazards will be isolated \ngusts to around 58mph and instances of sub-severe hail less than 1\". \n\nCome Monday and Tuesday, the upper low progresses east leaving the \nforecast area in a dry westerly flow regime keeping conditions calm \nto start the week. Regarding a return to a wetter pattern, ensemble \nguidance has shifted the onset somewhat with roughly 50% of all \nensembles supporting moisture return in some capacity to UT around \nthe Thursday-Friday timeframe. Roughly 80% of all ensemble members \nsupport moisture return, and subsequently precipitation by Saturday-\nSunday, though a notable shift has occurred within ensembles \nmuddying the forecast once again. This will be monitored and the \nforecast will more than likely change as a result going forward \ngiven low confidence.",
